      "text": "To find \\(\\sin A\\) in the right triangle \\(ABC\\) with \\(\\angle A = 90^\\circ\\), we need to identify the sides relative to \\(\\angle A\\). Since \\(\\angle A = 90^\\circ\\), the sides \\(AB\\) and \\(AC\\) are the legs, and \\(BC\\) is the hypotenuse. Therefore, \\(\\sin A\\) is defined as the ratio of the length of the opposite side to \\(\\angle A\\) (which is \\(AB\\)) to the length of the hypotenuse (\\(BC\\)).\n\nGiven:\n- \\(AB = 16\\)\n- \\(BC = 24\\)\n\nWe can calculate \\(\\sin A\\) as follows:\n\\[\n\\sin A = \\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} = \\frac{AB}{BC} = \\frac{16}{24}\n\\]\n\nNext, we simplify the fraction \\(\\frac{16}{24}\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{16}{24} = \\frac{16 \\div 8}{24 \\div 8} = \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\sin A\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{2}{3}}\n\\]",
