      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find \\(\\sin A\\) in the right triangle \\(ABC\\) with \\(\\angle A = 90^\\circ\\), we need to understand the properties of a right triangle. Since \\(\\angle A = 90^\\circ\\), the sine of \\(\\angle A\\) is defined as the ratio of the length of the side opposite to \\(\\angle A\\) to the hypotenuse.\n\nGiven:\n- \\(AB = 16\\)\n- \\(BC = 24\\)\n- \\(\\angle A = 90^\\circ\\)\n\nIn a right triangle, the side opposite to the right angle is the hypotenuse. Therefore, the hypotenuse \\(BC\\) is the side opposite to \\(\\angle A\\).\n\nThus, we need to find the length of \\(AC\\) (the side opposite to \\(\\angle A\\)) and use it to calculate \\(\\sin A\\).\n\nFirst, we use the Pythagorean theorem to find \\(AC\\):\n\\[\nAB^2 + AC^2 = BC^2\n\\]\nSubstituting the given values:\n\\[\n16^2 + AC^2 = 24^2\n\\]\n\\[\n256 + AC^2 = 576\n\\]\nSubtract 256 from both sides to solve for \\(AC^2\\):\n\\[\nAC^2 = 576 - 256\n\\]\n\\[\nAC^2 = 320\n\\]\nTaking the square root of both sides:\n\\[\nAC = \\sqrt{320} = \\sqrt{64 \\times 5} = 8\\sqrt{5}\n\\]\n\nNow that we have \\(AC = 8\\sqrt{5}\\), we can find \\(\\sin A\\):\n\\[\n\\sin A = \\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} = \\frac{AC}{BC} = \\frac{8\\sqrt{5}}{24}\n\\]\nSimplify the fraction:\n\\[\n\\sin A = \\frac{8\\sqrt{5}}{24} = \\frac{8}{24} \\cdot \\sqrt{5} = \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\sqrt{5} = \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\sin A\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}}\n\\]",
