      "text": "To find \\(\\sin A\\) in the right triangle \\(ABC\\) where \\(\\angle A = 90^\\circ\\), we start by identifying the sides of the triangle. In a right triangle with \\(\\angle A = 90^\\circ\\), the side opposite to \\(\\angle A\\) is \\(AB\\) and the hypotenuse is \\(BC\\). However, the problem states that \\(BC = 24\\) and \\(AB = 16\\), which implies that \\(CB\\) is the hypotenuse because \\(BC\\) is not the side opposite to \\(\\angle A\\).\n\nGiven:\n- \\(AB = 16\\)\n- \\(BC = 24\\)\n\nWe need to find \\(\\sin A\\). By definition, \\(\\sin A\\) is the ratio of the length of the opposite side to \\(\\angle A\\) (which is \\(AB\\)) to the length of the hypotenuse (\\(CB\\)).\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[\n\\sin A = \\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} = \\frac{AB}{BC} = \\frac{16}{24}\n\\]\n\nTo simplify \\(\\frac{16}{24}\\), we find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 16 and 24, which is 8. We divide both the numerator and the denominator by 8:\n\\[\n\\frac{16}{24} = \\frac{16 \\div 8}{24 \\div 8} = \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(\\sin A\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{2}{3}}\n\\]",
