      "text": "Since $\\triangle ABC$ is a right triangle with $\\angle A = 90^\\circ$, we have a right angle at $A$.  Thus, $\\sin A = \\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} = \\frac{BC}{AC}$.  We can use the Pythagorean Theorem to find $AC$:\n\\begin{align*} AC^2 &= BC^2 - AB^2 \\\\ &= 24^2 - 16^2 \\\\ &= 576 - 256 \\\\ &= 320 \\\\ &= 16\\sqrt{5} \\end{align*}So $\\sin A = \\frac{BC}{AC} = \\frac{24}{16\\sqrt{5}} = \\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{5}}$.  Rationalizing the denominator, we get $\\sin A = \\boxed{\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{10}}$.The answer is: \\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{10}",
